WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.160
Last time, we ended inside an active site, gripping the transition state hardest.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.060
But the famous picture of an enzyme is a lock cut for its key.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.680
That picture needs two fixes.

4
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.160
What are they?

5
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:25.033
In 1894, Emil Fischer explained why an enzyme acts on one sugar and ignores its

6
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:29.973
near-twin. His answer: enzyme and sugar fit like a lock and key.

7
00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:32.613
Fix one is about fit.

8
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.753
A rigid lock cut for the substrate would hold the substrate most tightly.

9
00:00:39.253 --> 00:00:44.813
Enzymes don't. Haldane and Pauling argued that the site fits the transition state

10
00:00:44.813 --> 00:00:45.813
best.

11
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:47.847
Fix two is about shape.

12
00:00:49.307 --> 00:00:50.347
Locks don't change shape.

13
00:00:51.627 --> 00:00:55.267
In 1958, Daniel Koshland proposed induced fit.

14
00:00:56.607 --> 00:01:00.107
His case was hexokinase, which moves a phosphate onto glucose.

15
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.950
Water carries the same OH group as glucose, and water is everywhere.

16
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.230
An open site should let water take the phosphate instead.

17
00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.150
It doesn't. Why not?

18
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:22.483
Because the open site is not ready.

19
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:27.643
Glucose binding closes it, bringing the catalytic groups into line.

20
00:01:28.923 --> 00:01:31.003
Water is too small to force that closing.

21
00:01:32.423 --> 00:01:33.763
Binding induces the fit.

22
00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:38.223
Crystal structures later bore this out for many enzymes.

23
00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:41.467
Later work added a second route.

24
00:01:42.707 --> 00:01:47.907
Many enzymes already shift between open and closed shapes before substrate arrives.

25
00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:50.927
The substrate picks the one that fits.

26
00:01:52.147 --> 00:01:53.887
That is conformational selection.

27
00:01:55.327 --> 00:01:57.047
Real enzymes often mix both.

28
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.620
Here is the one idea.

29
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.320
What an active site fits best, and whether it changes shape, are separate questions.

30
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.400
The answers: the transition state, and in many enzymes, yes.

31
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.660
The first answer reaches medicine.

32
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.220
Picture a molecule that binds the site more tightly than the substrate does.

33
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.200
If the enzyme cannot turn it over, it can shut the enzyme down.

34
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.200
Drugs shaped like a transition state exploit this.

35
00:02:29.183 --> 00:02:31.123
Statins get there another way.

36
00:02:32.263 --> 00:02:37.783
They block HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis.

37
00:02:39.223 --> 00:02:44.143
Part of each statin mimics the HMG part of the substrate and fills its pocket.

38
00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:49.727
Extra contacts nearby let it bind far more tightly than the substrate.

39
00:02:51.107 --> 00:02:55.447
Sitting in the active site and keeping the substrate out, it is a competitive

40
00:02:55.447 --> 00:03:01.807
inhibitor. Slowing the pathway makes the liver pull more LDL cholesterol from the

41
00:03:01.807 --> 00:03:02.807
blood.

42
00:03:03.117 --> 00:03:05.337
So ask two things of any active site.

43
00:03:06.597 --> 00:03:07.737
What is it built to fit?

44
00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:10.317
The fleeting top of the climb.

45
00:03:11.597 --> 00:03:14.717
Does it move? Locks never do.

46
00:03:16.017 --> 00:03:17.117
Many enzymes do.
